WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:11.939
&gt;&gt;

00:00:11.959 --> 00:00:13.939
[Piano music playing]

00:00:13.959 --> 00:00:14.939
&gt;&gt;

00:00:14.959 --> 00:00:15.959
[Pause]

00:00:15.959 --> 00:00:18.960
Robert: Hi. I'm Robert Scoble and I've been doing online

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:18.750
communities

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:23.419
since about 1984 and I have a story -- a historical story.

00:00:23.439 --> 00:00:26.439
I grew up here in the valley about a mile from Apple

00:00:25.969 --> 00:00:26.969
Computer and I

00:00:26.989 --> 00:00:30.929
belong to one of the Apple user groups that -- that was

00:00:30.949 --> 00:00:31.949
going and we

00:00:31.949 --> 00:00:36.309
had a friend -- a friend of the club, one of the people on

00:00:36.329 --> 00:00:37.329
the -- in

00:00:37.329 --> 00:00:40.809
the club had a garage and we turned it into a BDS.

00:00:40.829 --> 00:00:44.369
We had six computers with six 2400-baud modems that made

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:45.390
horrible

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:48.699
sounds when somebody called in, you know?

00:00:48.719 --> 00:00:49.969
And it was incredible.

00:00:49.989 --> 00:00:53.009
People could call in to these modems from around the world

00:00:53.029 --> 00:00:54.029
and trade a

00:00:54.029 --> 00:00:57.009
few files which were very, you know, nothing like facebook

00:00:57.029 --> 00:00:58.029
today;

00:00:58.029 --> 00:00:59.029
right?

00:00:58.279 --> 00:00:59.779
Just a few text files.

00:00:59.799 --> 00:01:04.370
So we would get e-mail from, you know, Korea, or Europe or

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:05.390
something

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:08.379
like that and it would be very asynchronous.

00:01:08.399 --> 00:01:11.399
We would have to wait a couple days for somebody to dial

00:01:10.989 --> 00:01:11.989
back in to

00:01:11.989 --> 00:01:15.239
get our e-mail over to the other half of the world; right?

00:01:14.989 --> 00:01:18.329
Because they would have to connect their BDSs around the

00:01:18.349 --> 00:01:19.349
world and

00:01:19.349 --> 00:01:22.400
join in, and call into our modems, and trade those text

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:23.849
files; and now

00:01:23.869 --> 00:01:28.409
we have YouTube where I can post hi-def video.

00:01:28.429 --> 00:01:31.679
I mean, I was at Loic's conference in Paris and I was

00:01:31.429 --> 00:01:32.640
uploading video

00:01:32.659 --> 00:01:37.230
in ten minutes that was 10 ADP hi-def video and putting it

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:38.250
out on the

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:40.750
internet and people around the world were watching it and

00:01:40.129 --> 00:01:41.129
interacting

00:01:41.129 --> 00:01:43.519
with it in live time.

00:01:43.539 --> 00:01:46.289
It was really, really incredible age we're at compared with

00:01:45.439 --> 00:01:46.439
the 1980s

00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:50.439
when things moved a lot slower than they do today.

00:01:50.459 --> 00:01:53.959
I actually that's why I wanted to pull up Google Buzz so you

00:01:52.789 --> 00:01:53.789
could

00:01:53.789 --> 00:01:55.039
just watch a little bit.

00:01:55.039 --> 00:01:57.920
I have something I'm following maybe 600 people right here.

00:01:57.939 --> 00:02:00.689
Let me just refresh this and make sure it's going.

00:02:00.519 --> 00:02:05.760
And this world is moving very, very quickly and so if you're

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:06.780
trying to

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:10.049
build a brand in this world and get noticed, if you're a

00:02:10.069 --> 00:02:11.069
musician, or

00:02:11.069 --> 00:02:15.009
a CEO, or a business, or a social project trying to help

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:16.030
Haiti or

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:19.679
whatnot, you're gonna have to compete with this world that

00:02:19.699 --> 00:02:20.699
just keeps

00:02:20.699 --> 00:02:23.949
moving at this speed and keep in mind I'm only seeing a

00:02:23.699 --> 00:02:24.699
very, very

00:02:24.699 --> 00:02:26.439
tiny slice of this world; right?

00:02:26.459 --> 00:02:29.459
There's 400 million people on facebook and I'm only seeing

00:02:29.409 --> 00:02:30.409
8600 people

00:02:30.419 --> 00:02:31.599
here on this screen.

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:35.789
So -- and you can sorta get a sense of it just keeps moving

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:36.810
all day,

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:38.559
all nigh.

00:02:38.579 --> 00:02:42.079
You know, you can get on at two in the morning and see, you

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:42.280
know,

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:44.780
twittering with this friends and but it just keeps moving

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:45.280
like this

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:46.280
all day long.

00:02:46.030 --> 00:02:47.030
Umm, hey, Scoble.

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:46.979
&gt;&gt;

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:47.780
[Laughing]

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:57.069
Robert: Yeah.

00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:58.840
And you guys can do this, too.

00:02:58.710 --> 00:02:58.909
&gt;&gt;

00:02:58.909 --> 00:02:59.909
[Laughing]

00:02:58.909 --> 00:02:59.109
&gt;&gt;

00:02:58.909 --> 00:02:59.909
[Clapping]

00:02:58.909 --> 00:03:01.909
Robert: Umm, and so I -- I just wanna put that up there

00:03:01.409 --> 00:03:02.409
because how

00:03:02.409 --> 00:03:05.409
do you get a business going in this kinda always on, always

00:03:04.789 --> 00:03:05.789
streaming,

00:03:05.789 --> 00:03:10.039
constantly increasing speed world; right?

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:13.979
I -- say start with four people, or maybe 40.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:15.999
ICQ is a good example of this.

00:03:16.019 --> 00:03:19.119
They released to 40 people on November 1st 1996.

00:03:19.139 --> 00:03:22.239
How many people here have heard of ICQ?

00:03:22.259 --> 00:03:23.789
Yeah.

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:27.209
So 40 people November 1st, 1996.

00:03:27.229 --> 00:03:30.479
I got on it because Chris Corillo, a friend of mine, told me

00:03:30.219 --> 00:03:31.219
oh, you

00:03:31.219 --> 00:03:33.339
gotta get on this cool new thing; right?

00:03:33.359 --> 00:03:36.559
And I joined around November 15th, which was six weeks

00:03:36.579 --> 00:03:37.579
later.

00:03:37.579 --> 00:03:42.809
I was the 65,361st person on the system; right?

00:03:42.829 --> 00:03:48.269
So in six weeks they went from 40 people to 65,000 people

00:03:48.289 --> 00:03:49.289
and that was

00:03:49.289 --> 00:03:50.289
in '96.

00:03:50.109 --> 00:03:53.309
So today everything has compressed.

00:03:53.329 --> 00:03:56.059
How does that happen?

00:03:56.079 --> 00:03:58.829
When I quit Microsoft -- so I worked at Microsoft for three

00:03:58.089 --> 00:03:59.089
years, I

00:03:59.089 --> 00:04:01.759
was a blogger and videographer there going around doing

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:02.780
interviews

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:04.459
with all sorts of people there.

00:04:04.479 --> 00:04:08.119
When I quit Microsoft I told 15 people at a conference

00:04:08.139 --> 00:04:09.139
smaller than

00:04:09.139 --> 00:04:12.739
this room and I told them to keep their mouth shut until

00:04:12.759 --> 00:04:13.759
Tuesday;

00:04:13.759 --> 00:04:14.759
right?

00:04:13.969 --> 00:04:17.819
This was a Saturday 'cause I hadn't told my boss that I was

00:04:17.839 --> 00:04:18.839
actually

00:04:18.839 --> 00:04:21.839
quit -- I mean, I was in discussions with him, he knew it

00:04:21.339 --> 00:04:22.339
was coming,

00:04:22.339 --> 00:04:25.589
but I hadn't actually made the call and said I'm quitting.

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:28.670
And of course it leaked but it leaked from a guy I didn't

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:29.900
even know.

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:33.170
It wasn't an A lister, it wasn't Mike Arrington, or Arianna

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:34.720
Huffington, or anybody like that.

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:37.350
It was a guy I didn't even know; right?

00:04:37.370 --> 00:04:39.160
And I know thousands of people.

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:42.149
I study this world every day.

00:04:42.169 --> 00:04:46.949
Wagner ***, of the Microsoft PR teams was tracking how many

00:04:46.969 --> 00:04:47.969
media hits

00:04:47.969 --> 00:04:51.840
they got and they said within three days, 50 million people

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:53.379
saw that I

00:04:53.399 --> 00:04:56.590
had quit Microsoft from those 15 conversations.

00:04:56.610 --> 00:05:00.110
I didn't do a press release, I didn't call up the wall

00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:00.310
Street Journal,

00:05:00.310 --> 00:05:05.060
I didn't try to build a, you know, a PR thing.

00:05:05.079 --> 00:05:07.829
I just told 15 people and they found the story interesting

00:05:07.669 --> 00:05:08.669
enough to

00:05:08.669 --> 00:05:09.919
tell their friends about it.

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:13.060
It leaked from a guy I didn't even know and the story

00:05:12.839 --> 00:05:13.839
started

00:05:13.839 --> 00:05:14.839
doubling.

00:05:14.269 --> 00:05:18.959
So it leaked 7:00 p.m. and every eight minutes it doubled.

00:05:18.979 --> 00:05:22.229
So it went from one blog, to two blogs, to four blogs, and

00:05:22.159 --> 00:05:23.159
when I hit

00:05:23.159 --> 00:05:26.410
about eight blogs I called my boss and I said I'm quitting '

00:05:26.430 --> 00:05:27.530
cause I

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:30.889
knew within 15 or 20 minutes he was gonna see it on his

00:05:30.909 --> 00:05:31.909
screens can

00:05:31.909 --> 00:05:34.909
then he would have even more pissed than he would have been

00:05:34.669 --> 00:05:35.669
had I not

00:05:35.669 --> 00:05:36.669
called him.

00:05:36.169 --> 00:05:40.280
Likely I caught him before he saw it but yeah.

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:43.050
It just kept doubling every few minutes just like ICQ kept

00:05:42.939 --> 00:05:43.939
doubling

00:05:43.939 --> 00:05:47.509
every few hours in the early days and then later it was

00:05:47.529 --> 00:05:48.529
doubling every

00:05:48.529 --> 00:05:50.509
17 days.

00:05:50.529 --> 00:05:51.529
Fun story.

00:05:51.219 --> 00:05:54.319
If you -- one of my favorite questions is, would you rather

00:05:54.339 --> 00:05:55.339
have a

00:05:55.339 --> 00:05:57.839
hundred thousand dollars today or would you rather have a

00:05:56.740 --> 00:05:57.740
penny that

00:05:57.740 --> 00:05:59.759
doubles every day for a month?

00:05:59.779 --> 00:06:03.029
To me an innovation is a penny that doubles every day or N

00:06:02.719 --> 00:06:03.719
days or

00:06:03.719 --> 00:06:06.259
doubles every N hours.

00:06:06.279 --> 00:06:09.579
The problem is, most people can't recognize that doubling

00:06:09.599 --> 00:06:10.599
penny until

00:06:10.599 --> 00:06:12.319
it hits about 20 doubles.

00:06:12.339 --> 00:06:16.000
So on day 20 -- you have about a thousand in your pocket.

00:06:16.019 --> 00:06:19.519
So if you know you're gonna die on day 21, take the hundred

00:06:18.609 --> 00:06:19.609
grand;

00:06:19.609 --> 00:06:20.609
right?

00:06:20.379 --> 00:06:23.379
All the money, all the money's due to compound in interest

00:06:22.519 --> 00:06:23.519
at the end

00:06:23.519 --> 00:06:27.019
of the month and you get five and a half or $10 million at

00:06:26.649 --> 00:06:27.649
the end of

00:06:27.649 --> 00:06:30.149
the month all due to compounding interest; right, which you

00:06:30.039 --> 00:06:31.039
guys all

00:06:31.039 --> 00:06:32.319
know.

00:06:32.339 --> 00:06:35.339
When I talked with Bill Gates and said go by this company

00:06:35.049 --> 00:06:36.049
called

00:06:36.049 --> 00:06:39.049
Flickr, go buy Skype -- this was before Skype got to E-bay -

00:06:38.879 --> 00:06:39.879
- I said,

00:06:39.879 --> 00:06:42.120
these companies are doubling pennies.

00:06:42.139 --> 00:06:44.209
You gotta own them, you know?

00:06:44.229 --> 00:06:47.980
This is what you want to go and acquire and the answer,

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:49.000
Steven

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:51.750
Sinofsky, who now runs Windows, wrote me an e-mail back

00:06:50.959 --> 00:06:51.959
about this

00:06:51.959 --> 00:06:54.860
biggy and it had the words "business value" repeated 13

00:06:54.879 --> 00:06:56.310
times. It was

00:06:56.329 --> 00:07:00.579
too early in the doubling stage for them to understand that

00:07:00.599 --> 00:07:01.599
it was

00:07:01.599 --> 00:07:02.720
doubling penny.

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:04.600
It was day ten; right?

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:05.870
It was a few dollars.

00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:05.870
&gt;&gt;

00:07:05.870 --> 00:07:06.870
[Laughing]

00:07:05.870 --> 00:07:08.370
Robert: And they didn't understand that these things were

00:07:07.629 --> 00:07:08.629
gonna

00:07:08.629 --> 00:07:11.199
continue doubling until they hut uh hundreds of millions of

00:07:11.219 --> 00:07:12.219
people and

00:07:12.219 --> 00:07:15.560
became businesses that are worth billions of dollars.

00:07:15.579 --> 00:07:22.269
So that's my lesson is I study how people work, how ideas

00:07:22.289 --> 00:07:23.310
spread from

00:07:23.329 --> 00:07:24.610
person to person.

00:07:24.629 --> 00:07:28.899
I, you know, if you come to my Twitter page, you'll see a

00:07:28.919 --> 00:07:29.919
list of tech

00:07:29.919 --> 00:07:35.629
company executives or innovators, or tech companies.

00:07:35.649 --> 00:07:38.899
Let's pick on tech company executives and see what they're

00:07:38.919 --> 00:07:39.919
writing

00:07:39.919 --> 00:07:41.139
right now.

00:07:41.159 --> 00:07:45.469
And these are executives that are everyone from a startup

00:07:45.489 --> 00:07:46.489
executive to

00:07:46.489 --> 00:07:50.569
an executive at Microsoft and you'll see what they wrote in

00:07:50.589 --> 00:07:51.889
realtime;

00:07:51.909 --> 00:07:54.909
and a lot of 'em I can tell you something about them.

00:07:54.779 --> 00:07:59.300
Like Sacca is an investor in twitter, you know, Jeremy

00:07:59.319 --> 00:08:01.230
Toeman launched

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:04.850
five companies at CES and there's one investor of CES I don'

00:08:04.870 --> 00:08:05.870
t know,

00:08:05.870 --> 00:08:07.509
four times I think.

00:08:07.529 --> 00:08:10.279
And so I start learning by reading these people and meeting

00:08:09.819 --> 00:08:10.819
them

00:08:10.819 --> 00:08:13.819
face-to-face I start learning about who they are and what

00:08:13.689 --> 00:08:14.689
kinds of

00:08:14.689 --> 00:08:18.339
thins they want to pass along the their friends; right?

00:08:18.359 --> 00:08:20.609
So I know Jeremy Toeman's totally into gadgets.

00:08:20.409 --> 00:08:24.370
He launched Bug Labs, which is a totally innovative company.

00:08:24.389 --> 00:08:27.199
Helped launch Slingbox which many of you have.

00:08:27.219 --> 00:08:30.990
Umm, and so I know he wants to hear about gadgets or see

00:08:31.009 --> 00:08:32.009
innovative

00:08:32.009 --> 00:08:35.960
companies in the gadget space or there's a certain kinda

00:08:35.980 --> 00:08:36.980
thing, you

00:08:36.980 --> 00:08:37.980
know?

00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:40.350
Compare that to MC Hammer he wants to hear a different kinda

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:41.240
innovation.

00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:45.600
He wants to hear about music and entrepreneur innovation.

00:08:45.620 --> 00:08:48.620
Maybe he has some of the same interests that Jeremy does but

00:08:48.549 --> 00:08:49.549
I start

00:08:49.549 --> 00:08:52.720
looking at how to create a -- how to pass along a story.

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:57.000
Umm, when I first sold Seagate, so Seagate was one of my

00:08:57.019 --> 00:08:58.019
sponsors for

00:08:58.019 --> 00:09:01.419
a couple years, I told -- I went in there say said, let's

00:09:01.439 --> 00:09:02.439
not talk

00:09:02.439 --> 00:09:04.939
about twitter, or face book, or whatever's going on.

00:09:04.799 --> 00:09:06.860
I wanna hear what is your story?

00:09:06.879 --> 00:09:10.840
How do you want to change your story to the world?

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:14.289
And that started a much better conversation about what to

00:09:14.309 --> 00:09:15.309
do.

00:09:15.309 --> 00:09:18.559
Don't -- my first advice is, don't get caught up in these

00:09:18.189 --> 00:09:19.189
tools 'cause

00:09:19.189 --> 00:09:20.590
they're just tools.

00:09:20.610 --> 00:09:24.389
Get caught up in what's the story you want to tell?

00:09:24.409 --> 00:09:26.659
Do you wanna raise a million dollars for Haiti?

00:09:26.449 --> 00:09:27.990
How do you do that?

00:09:28.009 --> 00:09:31.259
Who are the people that you want to touch and get in touch

00:09:31.250 --> 00:09:32.250
with;

00:09:32.250 --> 00:09:33.250
right?

00:09:32.689 --> 00:09:36.309
And then you need to study, you know, start with one of

00:09:36.329 --> 00:09:37.329
them, like

00:09:37.329 --> 00:09:40.340
Pierre Omidyar, which Loik knows, he started E-bay.

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:41.860
He has a lot of money.

00:09:41.860 --> 00:09:45.110
So if you're trying to raise money, go where the money is;

00:09:44.559 --> 00:09:45.559
right?

00:09:45.559 --> 00:09:48.059
Maybe you know -- Loic knows him because Loic is following

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:49.000
him on

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:50.000
twitter; right?

00:09:49.909 --> 00:09:54.799
So study who's following him, and study who's going along,

00:09:54.819 --> 00:09:56.379
and build

00:09:56.399 --> 00:09:58.200
the -- go take Loic out to lunch.

00:09:58.220 --> 00:09:59.939
Learn something about Loic first.

00:09:59.959 --> 00:10:03.139
&gt;&gt; That's two hours.

00:10:03.159 --> 00:10:04.159
Robert: Two hours.

00:10:03.909 --> 00:10:04.909
Yeah.

00:10:04.159 --> 00:10:04.360
&gt;&gt;

00:10:04.159 --> 00:10:05.159
[Laughing]

00:10:04.159 --> 00:10:07.259
Robert: And buy him a nice bottle of wine 'cause he likes

00:10:07.279 --> 00:10:08.279
good

00:10:08.279 --> 00:10:09.279
wine --

00:10:08.529 --> 00:10:08.730
&gt;&gt;

00:10:08.529 --> 00:10:09.529
[Laughing]

00:10:08.529 --> 00:10:11.549
Robert: -- and then he might introduce you to Pierre which

00:10:11.569 --> 00:10:12.569
might --

00:10:12.569 --> 00:10:16.759
which will be a much stronger relationship than if you just

00:10:16.779 --> 00:10:17.779
tried to

00:10:17.779 --> 00:10:21.529
e-mail pear out of the blue and he doesn't know who you are.

00:10:20.319 --> 00:10:23.179
This is how I got most of my interviews at Microsoft.

00:10:23.199 --> 00:10:26.419
I would start with one guy that I knew, interviewed him, and

00:10:26.439 --> 00:10:27.439
I would

00:10:27.439 --> 00:10:29.700
ask him, well, who else should I interview?

00:10:29.720 --> 00:10:32.279
Oh, there's another guy over in research.

00:10:32.299 --> 00:10:33.470
You should interview him.

00:10:33.490 --> 00:10:36.740
Well, can you introduce me 'cause if I just call him, he's

00:10:35.919 --> 00:10:36.919
gonna go,

00:10:36.919 --> 00:10:37.919
you're a jerk.

00:10:37.669 --> 00:10:40.919
I'm not -- try to think about trying to do what I did.

00:10:40.409 --> 00:10:45.539
So at Microsoft I'd either be 600 employees company-wide;

00:10:45.559 --> 00:10:46.559
right?

00:10:46.559 --> 00:10:51.139
I interviewed everybody from Bill Gates down to the janitor.

00:10:51.159 --> 00:10:53.909
